{"reason": "The initial plan focuses on adding cross-attention fusion and differentiated learning rates to better utilize semantic embeddings. Historical experiments show that similar feature-rich architectures with transformer fusion can improve log loss from ~0.85 to ~0.70-0.75, but a simpler MLP approach (as in the current code) achieved ~0.85 validation log loss. The current transformer encoder with stacked tokens underperforms because it treats all three modalities equally. Therefore, I refine the model_design to implement cross-attention and differentiated LRs, and also refine training_evaluation to adjust optimizer parameter groups. Data processing remains unchanged as features are already well-engineered.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "1) Replace the simple stack-and-pool transformer encoder with a Cross-Modal Attention (CMA) fusion block: use semantic embeddings as query (Q) and concatenated dense+sparse features as key/value (K/V) in a multi-head attention layer, followed by residual connection, layer normalization, and a lightweight MLP head. 2) Keep the three projection heads (proj_dense, proj_sem, proj_sparse) but output to the same d_model dimension (256). 3) The forward method will compute cross-attention between the semantic token and the dense/sparse token, then classify via a small MLP. This change exploits the natural inductive bias that semantic context should query stylometric patterns, similar to successful historical experiments that used attention for modality fusion.", "training_evaluation": "1) Implement differentiated learning rates: separate optimizer parameter groups for the semantic projection branch (lr=5e-3) and the dense/sparse projection branches plus the CMA fusion and classifier (lr=5e-4). Use AdamW with weight_decay=0.01. 2) Keep all other training hyperparameters unchanged (batch size 32, epochs 50, warmup ratio 0.1, gradient clipping, label smoothing, early stopping). 3) This differential LR mimics fine-tuning strategies that worked in historical experiments, allowing the semantic branch to adapt quickly while preserving handcrafted features."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan focuses on adding cross-attention fusion and differentiated learning rates to better utilize semantic embeddings. Historical experiments show that similar feature-rich architectures with transformer fusion can improve log loss from ~0.85 to ~0.70-0.75, but a simpler MLP approach (as in the current code) achieved ~0.85 validation log loss. The current transformer encoder with stacked tokens underperforms because it treats all three modalities equally. Therefore, I refine the model_design to implement cross-attention and differentiated LRs, and also refine training_evaluation to adjust optimizer parameter groups. Data processing remains unchanged as features are already well-engineered.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "1) Replace the simple stack-and-pool transformer encoder with a Cross-Modal Attention (CMA) fusion block: use semantic embeddings as query (Q) and concatenated dense+sparse features as key/value (K/V) in a multi-head attention layer, followed by residual connection, layer normalization, and a lightweight MLP head. 2) Keep the three projection heads (proj_dense, proj_sem, proj_sparse) but output to the same d_model dimension (256). 3) The forward method will compute cross-attention between the semantic token and the dense/sparse token, then classify via a small MLP. This change exploits the natural inductive bias that semantic context should query stylometric patterns, similar to successful historical experiments that used attention for modality fusion.",
    "training_evaluation": "1) Implement differentiated learning rates: separate optimizer parameter groups for the semantic projection branch (lr=5e-3) and the dense/sparse projection branches plus the CMA fusion and classifier (lr=5e-4). Use AdamW with weight_decay=0.01. 2) Keep all other training hyperparameters unchanged (batch size 32, epochs 50, warmup ratio 0.1, gradient clipping, label smoothing, early stopping). 3) This differential LR mimics fine-tuning strategies that worked in historical experiments, allowing the semantic branch to adapt quickly while preserving handcrafted features."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan's core direction is to replace the handcrafted feature pipeline with a fine-tuned DeBERTa-v3-base transformer model for end-to-end author classification. Historical experiments show that feature-rich architectures with cross-attention fusion can improve log loss, but they still rely on handcrafted features, which are inferior to a fine-tuned pretrained language model. The current implementation already includes some of those improvements (cross-attention, differentiated LRs) but still uses pre-extracted features, limiting performance. Therefore, the refinement focuses on data_processing_and_feature_engineering (to stream to raw text for the new model) and model_design (to implement the DeBERTa-v3-base transformer), keeping training_evaluation mostly unchanged (to reuse proven training loop, loss, scheduler, and early stopping).", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Replace the multi-feature engineering pipeline (stylometric, readability, POS, n-grams, semantic embeddings, etc.) with a minimal preprocessing step that only tokenizes raw text for the DeBERTa-v3 tokenizer. Specifically, remove all feature extraction functions and scalers. Keep the stratified train-validation split and label encoding. Store raw text in the dataset instead of precomputed features. This change is necessary because the initial plan's core is to drop handcrafted features and use a fine-tuned transformer; historical experiments confirm that even with cross-attention fusion, handcrafted features plateau compared to transformer-based representations. Implementation: modify the data loading to only keep X_train_texts, X_val_texts, y_train_labels, y_val_labels as raw strings and labels; remove all code for train_dense, train_semantic_emb, train_sparse, etc. The DataLoader will return text strings and labels, tokenized on the fly inside the model forward pass.", "model_design": "Replace the CrossModalAttentionFusion model with a fine-tuned DeBERTa-v3-base transformer for sequence classification. Use `AutoModelForSequenceClassification.from_pretrained('microsoft/deberta-v3-base', num_labels=3)` and add a dropout layer before the classifier head. The forward method will tokenize the raw text using the DeBERTa tokenizer (with padding, truncation, max_length=512) and return logits. Keep the same loss function (CrossEntropyLoss with label_smoothing=0.1), optimizer (AdamW), and learning rate schedule as in the initial plan (lr=2e-5 for all parameters, weight_decay=0.01, warmup ratio=0.1). This change aligns with the initial plan's direction to use a fine-tuned transformer; historical experiments show that DeBERTa-v3 significantly outperforms feature-based methods on author identification. Implementation: define a new class `DebertaAuthorClassifier` that loads the pretrained model and a tokenizer. Remove the old model class entirely. The model will directly take text strings as input, tokenize them, and compute logits without any handcrafted features."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan's core direction is to replace the handcrafted feature pipeline with a fine-tuned DeBERTa-v3-base transformer model for end-to-end author classification. Historical experiments show that feature-rich architectures with cross-attention fusion can improve log loss, but they still rely on handcrafted features, which are inferior to a fine-tuned pretrained language model. The current implementation already includes some of those improvements (cross-attention, differentiated LRs) but still uses pre-extracted features, limiting performance. Therefore, the refinement focuses on data_processing_and_feature_engineering (to stream to raw text for the new model) and model_design (to implement the DeBERTa-v3-base transformer), keeping training_evaluation mostly unchanged (to reuse proven training loop, loss, scheduler, and early stopping).",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Replace the multi-feature engineering pipeline (stylometric, readability, POS, n-grams, semantic embeddings, etc.) with a minimal preprocessing step that only tokenizes raw text for the DeBERTa-v3 tokenizer. Specifically, remove all feature extraction functions and scalers. Keep the stratified train-validation split and label encoding. Store raw text in the dataset instead of precomputed features. This change is necessary because the initial plan's core is to drop handcrafted features and use a fine-tuned transformer; historical experiments confirm that even with cross-attention fusion, handcrafted features plateau compared to transformer-based representations. Implementation: modify the data loading to only keep X_train_texts, X_val_texts, y_train_labels, y_val_labels as raw strings and labels; remove all code for train_dense, train_semantic_emb, train_sparse, etc. The DataLoader will return text strings and labels, tokenized on the fly inside the model forward pass.",
    "model_design": "Replace the CrossModalAttentionFusion model with a fine-tuned DeBERTa-v3-base transformer for sequence classification. Use `AutoModelForSequenceClassification.from_pretrained('microsoft/deberta-v3-base', num_labels=3)` and add a dropout layer before the classifier head. The forward method will tokenize the raw text using the DeBERTa tokenizer (with padding, truncation, max_length=512) and return logits. Keep the same loss function (CrossEntropyLoss with label_smoothing=0.1), optimizer (AdamW), and learning rate schedule as in the initial plan (lr=2e-5 for all parameters, weight_decay=0.01, warmup ratio=0.1). This change aligns with the initial plan's direction to use a fine-tuned transformer; historical experiments show that DeBERTa-v3 significantly outperforms feature-based methods on author identification. Implementation: define a new class `DebertaAuthorClassifier` that loads the pretrained model and a tokenizer. Remove the old model class entirely. The model will directly take text strings as input, tokenize them, and compute logits without any handcrafted features."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results show clear overfitting: DeBERTa training loss drops to 0.06 while validation loss increases to 0.79, and the ensemble val log loss is 0.2553. The initial plan directly addresses overfitting via multi-sample dropout, cosine annealing with restarts, increased dropout, and SWA. Similar historical experiments succeeded by simplifying to a pure transformer pipeline and focusing on regularization. Therefore, refinements concentrate on model_design to implement multi-sample dropout and increase dropout rate, and training_evaluation to replace the scheduler, add SWA, and adjust early stopping. Data processing is kept unchanged since handcrafted features are already used and historical plans showed that removing them may not be optimal given time constraints.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Modify the DeBERTa model class to add Multi-Sample Dropout (MSD) in the classifier head. Instead of a single dropout, apply dropout K=4 times during training (forward method when training), average the resulting logits, and compute loss on averaged logits. Increase DROPOUT from 0.2 to 0.3. The model design changes require overriding the forward method to loop over dropout masks, accumulating logits, and averaging them. This directly addresses overfitting observed in execution (training loss 0.06, val loss 0.79) by providing a Monte Carlo estimate of the expected output, as successful historical experiments with DeBERTa-v3-large showed improvement with MSD.", "training_evaluation": "Replace the linear warmup scheduler with CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts (T_0=4, T_mult=2). Implement Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) after training: after early stopping triggers, continue training for 5 more epochs with a constant low lr (1e-6) and average the model weights. Keep early stopping patience at 5 but monitor validation log loss. Also, implement the custom loss calculation based on averaged logits from MSD. This change leverages historical success of cosine annealing with restarts to escape overfitted minima and SWA to find flatter minima, improving generalization as indicated by the current overfitting pattern."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show clear overfitting: DeBERTa training loss drops to 0.06 while validation loss increases to 0.79, and the ensemble val log loss is 0.2553. The initial plan directly addresses overfitting via multi-sample dropout, cosine annealing with restarts, increased dropout, and SWA. Similar historical experiments succeeded by simplifying to a pure transformer pipeline and focusing on regularization. Therefore, refinements concentrate on model_design to implement multi-sample dropout and increase dropout rate, and training_evaluation to replace the scheduler, add SWA, and adjust early stopping. Data processing is kept unchanged since handcrafted features are already used and historical plans showed that removing them may not be optimal given time constraints.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Modify the DeBERTa model class to add Multi-Sample Dropout (MSD) in the classifier head. Instead of a single dropout, apply dropout K=4 times during training (forward method when training), average the resulting logits, and compute loss on averaged logits. Increase DROPOUT from 0.2 to 0.3. The model design changes require overriding the forward method to loop over dropout masks, accumulating logits, and averaging them. This directly addresses overfitting observed in execution (training loss 0.06, val loss 0.79) by providing a Monte Carlo estimate of the expected output, as successful historical experiments with DeBERTa-v3-large showed improvement with MSD.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the linear warmup scheduler with CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts (T_0=4, T_mult=2). Implement Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) after training: after early stopping triggers, continue training for 5 more epochs with a constant low lr (1e-6) and average the model weights. Keep early stopping patience at 5 but monitor validation log loss. Also, implement the custom loss calculation based on averaged logits from MSD. This change leverages historical success of cosine annealing with restarts to escape overfitted minima and SWA to find flatter minima, improving generalization as indicated by the current overfitting pattern."
