{"reason": "The execution shows DeBERTa severely underfits (val log loss 0.7947 at epoch 1, rising to 0.8633 at final evaluation) and the ensemble relies 80% on XGBoost, wasting the transformer's potential. The initial plan's core is to fix DeBERTa training via a two-phase approach with discriminative learning rates, cosine annealing, and richer embeddings (multi-layer pooling). Similar successful historical experiments (Plan 1) replaced handcrafted features with a pure transformer pipeline, but here the handcrafted features are already present and DeBERTa is added. Failed experiments (Plan 2) showed that simple linear warmup and single-epoch fine-tuning cannot harness DeBERTa. Therefore, I focus on model_design to implement the two-phase training, enhanced pooling, and focal loss; training_evaluation to adopt cosine annealing with warm restarts; and data_processing_and_feature_engineering to replace the current tokenizer encoding with on-the-fly tokenization in the DataLoader to support dynamic features.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation", "data_processing_and_feature_engineering"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Modify data loading to prepare training, validation, and test datasets that store raw text strings and labels, and a new dataset class that tokenizes on-the-fly using the DeBERTa tokenizer with padding, truncation, and max_length=256. Remove pre-tokenized encodings and TensorDatasets. This enables dynamic tokenization and reduces memory usage. The change aligns with the initial plan's need to handle variable-length inputs and support multi-layer pooling.", "model_design": "Replace the current DeBERTa model class with one implementing a two-phase training strategy: Phase 1 freezes the encoder and trains only the classification head with a higher learning rate (5e-4) for 5 epochs; Phase 2 unfreezes all layers and fine-tunes with discriminative learning rates (encoder 1e-5, classifier 1e-4). Use Focal Loss (gamma=2.0) with temperature-based confidence penalty (T=2.0). For feature extraction, implement weighted average pooling across all token embeddings and concatenate [CLS] embedding (2048-dim), plus extract hidden states from layers [-1,-2,-3] and mean-pool them (3072-dim). This directly addresses the underfitting and enriches representations for XGBoost.", "training_evaluation": "Replace the linear warmup scheduler with CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts (T_0=5, T_mult=2) and add a gradient clipping value of 3.0. Instead of saving based on cross-entropy loss, compute and save the model with best validation log loss. After Phase 2, implement test-time adaptation: pseudo-label the top 20% most confident test predictions and re-optimize ensemble weights on this pseudo-labeled set. This leverages successful historical experiments that improved calibration and ensemble performance."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution shows DeBERTa severely underfits (val log loss 0.7947 at epoch 1, rising to 0.8633 at final evaluation) and the ensemble relies 80% on XGBoost, wasting the transformer's potential. The initial plan's core is to fix DeBERTa training via a two-phase approach with discriminative learning rates, cosine annealing, and richer embeddings (multi-layer pooling). Similar successful historical experiments (Plan 1) replaced handcrafted features with a pure transformer pipeline, but here the handcrafted features are already present and DeBERTa is added. Failed experiments (Plan 2) showed that simple linear warmup and single-epoch fine-tuning cannot harness DeBERTa. Therefore, I focus on model_design to implement the two-phase training, enhanced pooling, and focal loss; training_evaluation to adopt cosine annealing with warm restarts; and data_processing_and_feature_engineering to replace the current tokenizer encoding with on-the-fly tokenization in the DataLoader to support dynamic features.",
